  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nMCS and remnant MCV continue to trek southeast across central Kansas \nthis afternoon. Increased northwesterly flow aloft and several \nembedded perturbations in vicinity of an outflow boundary may \nprovide convergence for an isolated shower or storm in the late \nafternoon. Most CAMs are inconsistent on coverage and location of \nisolated convection as overall confidence in development is low. \nHowever with the decaying MCV present, have added slight pops across \neastern KS into the late afternoon. Isolated severe storms could \nproduce damaging wind gusts and large hail given the marginal deep \nlayer shear and MUCAPE ~3000 J/KG, if they were to form. Overnight \nconvection is still in question for north central KS as the some of \nthe 12Z suite in guidance is trending further west/south with the \nMCS. Environment ahead of the cold front is supportive of severe \nstorms as MUCAPE is around 3000 J/KG, if the MCS begins to develop \nin northern KS. Maintained low chance pops for now given the cold \nfront moving through. \n\nAdded a very low chance for showers and storms cannot be ruled out \nalong the frontal boundary on Wednesday, especially south of I-35 in \nthe afternoon. Drier, sfc high pressure settling southward should \nresult in a pleasant, less humid Wednesday evening with dewpoints \nnear 60 and temps in the low 70s. High pressure sticks around \nThursday before shifting eastward as mid level heights increase, \nleading to southerly winds and warmer temperatures this weekend. \nHeat indices peak in the low 100s Sunday and Monday as south winds \npickup from 15 to 20 mph sustained. The upper ridge flattens on \nSunday as a northern stream shortwave trough pushes a cold front \nthrough the central plains. Ensembles exhibit a wide variance on \ntiming and location of the front and subsequent convection through \nTuesday. Broad low chances seem reasonable as the front meanders in \nthe area.",
